@@s001 Functions as values
@@s002 ora:element under examination,ok:kept,no:discarded
@@s003 <code>List(1, -2, 3).filter(x =&gt; x &lt; 0)</code>. I write only the rule (the predicate); the loop is done by filter.
@@s004 x = 1: 1 &lt; 0 is <b>false</b>. Discarded.
@@s005 x = −2: −2 &lt; 0 is <b>true</b>. It stays.
@@s006 x = 3: false. Discarded.
@@s007 The result is a <b>new</b> list, List(−2). The starting one has not changed.
@@s008 dato:literal of data,ora:literal of function,ok:result
@@s009 ora:h (head),ok:p(h) = true,no:p(h) = false,spento:already discarded
@@s010 ora:current form,ok:result,no:does not compile
@@s011 ora:the factory,due:function produced,ok:kept,no:discarded
@@s012 Functions as values
@@s013 In Scala a function is a value like <code>42</code> or <code>"ciao"</code>: you put it in a <code>val</code>, pass it to another function, return it. A function that receives or returns functions is called <b>higher-order</b> (higher-order function, HOF).
@@s014 Writing a value, writing a function
@@s015 For data you have <b>literals</b>: you write the value directly in the code.
@@s016 For functions too there is a literal: the <b>anonymous function</b> (or lambda). It has no name; it gets one only if you put it in a <code>val</code>.
@@s017 Reading and using an anonymous function
@@s018 To the left of the arrow the parameter, with its type.
@@s019 The arrow separates "what comes in" from "what I compute".
@@s020 On the right the body. Its value is the result. The type of <code>negative</code> is <code>Int => Boolean</code>.
@@s021 You call it like a normal function.
@@s022 <code>x</code> is replaced by <code>-42</code> in the body.
@@s023 The result.
@@s024 Literals of data and literals of functions
@@s025 Passing a function to another
@@s026 <code>filter</code> is a method of lists that receives a "yes/no" function and keeps only the elements for which the answer is yes. <b>Whoever calls your function is <code>filter</code></b>, once per element.
@@s027 List(1, -2, 3).filter(x => x < 0)
@@s028 I write only the rule. I write no loop.
@@s029 <code>filter</code> calls the function on the first element: <code>x</code> takes the value 1. False: 1 is discarded.
@@s030 Second element: true, -2 stays.
@@s031 Third element: discarded.
@@s032 The result is a <b>new</b> list. The starting one has not changed.
@@s033 filter, one element at a time
@@s034 Frequent doubt: where does x come from?
@@s035 <code>x</code> does not exist before. It is the <b>name of the parameter</b> of the function you wrote. The value is given by whoever calls it, here <code>filter</code>. It is the same thing that happens when you call <code>abs(-3)</code>: inside <code>abs</code>, <code>n</code> is worth -3.
@@s036 <code>p</code> against <code>p(h)</code>
@@s037 When a function receives another function as a parameter, inside the body you can do two different things with that name. It is your solution of <code>dropWhile</code> (week 2).
@@s038 dropWhile: remove elements while the condition holds
@@s039 <code>p</code> is a parameter of type <code>A => Boolean</code>: a function that receives an element and answers true or false. Whoever calls <code>dropWhile</code> decides which.
@@s040 If the list is not empty, <code>h</code> is the first element and <code>t</code> the rest.
@@s041 <code>p(h)</code> <b>runs</b> the function on the first element. The result is a <code>Boolean</code>, perfect for the <code>if</code>.
@@s042 In the recursive call I pass <code>p</code> <b>without parentheses</b>: I do not run it, I hand it whole to the next call, which will use it on the next element.
@@s043 As soon as an element does not satisfy <code>p</code>, I return <code>l</code> as it is: that list already starts from the right element.
@@s044 I write
@@s045 What it is
@@s046 Type
@@s047 the whole function, to pass on
@@s048 <code>A => Boolean</code>
@@s049 the result of the function on <code>h</code>
@@s050 <code>Boolean</code>
@@s051 dropWhile: pass p, run p(h)
@@s052 Three ways of writing the same function
@@s053 Scala lets you shorten anonymous functions when it understands the types by itself.
@@s054 From the longest to the shortest
@@s055 Full version, with the parameter's type.
@@s056 <code>l</code> is a <code>List[Int]</code>, so Scala already knows that <code>x</code> is an <code>Int</code>: the type can be removed.
@@s057 If the parameter appears <b>only once</b>, you can put <code>_</code> in its place. No name, no arrow.
@@s058 Each <code>_</code> is a <b>new</b> parameter, in order. Two underscores = two parameters.
@@s059 A function with two arguments really is needed (accumulator and element): <code>_ + _</code> is fine.
@@s060 From the full form to _
@@s061 Functions that return functions
@@s062 A function can also <b>produce</b> a function. It is the basis of currying, on the next page.
@@s063 A factory of functions
@@s064 The return type is <code>Int => Boolean</code>: <code>piuDi</code> returns a function.
@@s065 I call the factory with 100.
@@s066 <code>grande</code> is the function with the threshold already fixed inside.
@@s067 And I use it where a function is needed.
@@s068 piuDi(100): a factory of functions
